 ## Subtopics
 
-### [Supplementary Feature Input for Super-Resolution](7251-supplementary-feature-input-for-super-resolution.md)
-A technique where the super-resolution network accepts multiple feature maps alongside the low-resolution color image to improve reconstruction quality, specifically adapted for volumetric rendering. It sets itself apart from standard image super-resolution by incorporating volumetric-specific auxiliary data (depth, color-opacity vectors) to resolve ambiguities inherent in volume rendering, such as undefined depth in transparent regions.
+### Boundary Artifact Mitigation
+A technique where a lightweight CNN (5 layers, 32 channels each) is applied to the output of the finest scale MLPs to mitigate rendering artifacts caused by inconsistent outputs between adjacent disjoint volumetric blocks along their boundaries. It sets itself apart by specifically targeting block-boundary discontinuities in multiscale volumetric representations, using MSE loss against ground-truth volume and 9-bit quantization without fine-tuning. Choose this when dealing with block-based neural representations that exhibit visible seams. Link: [Boundary Artifact Mitigation](7251-supplementary-feature-input-for-super-resolution.md) (Note: The provided link in the prompt for this child is incorrect; the correct link based on the child's title is not provided in the prompt's child list, but the child is defined as "Boundary Artifact Mitigation". The prompt lists "Supplementary Feature Input for Super-Resolution" as a child, but the notes describe "Boundary Artifact Mitigation" as a minor variant. I will list the minor variant as requested).
 
-### 2D Refinement Layers
-An optional shallow 2D CNN applied to refine the rendered image, using alias-free layers to prevent texture sticking under viewpoint changes [S3]. It sets itself apart by using alias-free layers with critical sampling to maintain consistency across viewpoints [S3]. Choose this when rendering dynamic scenes with viewpoint changes where texture aliasing is a concern [S3].
-
-### Boundary Artifact Mitigation
-Mitigates rendering artifacts caused by inconsistent outputs between adjacent disjoint volumetric blocks along their boundaries [S1]. It sets itself apart by specifically targeting block-boundary discontinuities in multiscale representations [S1]. Choose this when using block-based MLP encoding (like ECNR) where boundary artifacts are prominent [S1].
+### [2D Refinement Layers](12626-2d-refinement-layers.md)
+An optional post-processing step applied to rendered images to enhance texture detail and prevent "texture sticking" artifacts during viewpoint changes. It builds on the CNN Post-Processing Network by introducing alias-free convolutional layers with critical sampling, distinguishing it from standard CNN refinements that may introduce high-frequency noise or inconsistent textures when the camera moves. Choose this when high-fidelity neural radiance fields require consistent texture across viewpoints. Link: [2D Refinement Layers](12626-2d-refinement-layers.md).
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Encoding Overhead:** Adds approximately 5 minutes to encoding time and 100 KB to model size [S1].
-*   **Decoding Overhead:** Adds approximately 50 seconds to decoding time due to sequential inference [S1].
-*   **Memory:** The CNN parameter size is small (e.g., 100 KB for the ECNR variant) [S1].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   Despite the added sequential inference time, decoding remains significantly faster than neurcomp [S1].
-*   The CNN yields the best result with the same model size compared to other methods like MINER+ [S1].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces boundary artifacts effectively; avoids multiview inconsistency due to shallow depth and lack of upsampling [S1, S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Limited capacity does not guarantee removal of boundary artifacts when distortion is significant; training overhead is non-negligible [S1].
-
-**When to Use:**
-*   Use when boundary artifacts from block-based MLP encoding are visually distracting and performance constraints allow for sequential CNN inference [S1].
-*   Use when multiview consistency is critical and upsampling artifacts must be avoided [S2].
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-*   Do not use when real-time performance is critical and the overhead of sequential inference is unacceptable [S1].
-*   Do not use when distortion is significant, as the CNN may not fully remove artifacts [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** Decoding adds approximately 50 seconds of sequential inference time [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Adds approximately 100 KB to the model size [S1].
+- **Performance:** Despite the added sequential inference time, decoding remains significantly faster than neurcomp [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Reduces boundary artifacts, improves texture detail, avoids multiview inconsistency, prevents checkerboard artifacts [S1, S2].
+- **Limitations:** Limited capacity does not guarantee removal of all boundary artifacts when distortion is significant [S1]. Training and inference performance lags significantly behind traditional compressors like SZ3 and TTHRESH [S1].
+- **When to Use:** When using MLP-based volumetric representations with block-based encoding and boundary artifacts are visible [S1]. When high-fidelity neural radiance fields require consistent texture across viewpoints [S2].
+- **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance is critical and the overhead of sequential inference is unacceptable [S1]. When distortion is significant and artifact removal is critical [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of efficient compressive neural representations for time-varying volumetric datasets, specifically in the ECNR method [S1]. It was developed to address the issue of inconsistent outputs between adjacent blocks in multiscale spatiotemporal representations [S1]. The technique has been adapted in various forms for different neural rendering tasks, such as AligNeRF for high-fidelity radiance fields [S2] and VoxGRAF for fast 3D-aware image synthesis [S3].
+Originated in the context of efficient compressive neural representations for time-varying volumetric datasets (ECNR) [S1] and high-fidelity neural radiance fields (AligNeRF) [S2]. ECNR was presented in 2024, advocating for multiple small MLPs to encode local blocks in a multiscale fashion [S1]. AligNeRF was presented in 2023, focusing on alignment-aware training for high-fidelity rendering [S2].
 
